    <body>
      <sec id="article-24813.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Mechanical back strain is a prevalent condition that involves injury to the muscles, ligaments, or tendons of the lower back, often resulting from overuse, sudden movements, or improper body mechanics. Most patients who develop a mechanical back strain experience a benign course with resolution of symptoms; however, a subset may continue to experience chronic, disabling pain that impacts daily functioning. Evaluation and management are typically conservative, including rest, activity modification, physical therapy, and ergonomic interventions. Healthcare professionals must carefully differentiate mechanical back strain from other causes of back pain and remain vigilant for life-threatening etiologies. Understanding the etiology, epidemiology, clinical evaluation, differential diagnoses, and treatment strategies is critical for effectively managing and preventing complications.</p>
        <p>Through this course, participants comprehensively understand mechanical back strain, including its presentation, risk factors, and evidence-based treatment approaches. The activity emphasizes the importance of collaboration within an interprofessional team, demonstrating how coordinated care among primary clinicians, physical therapists, nurses, and other healthcare professionals enhances patient outcomes. By sharing expertise and communication across disciplines, the team can ensure timely evaluation, implement individualized management plans, and monitor progress to reduce the risk of chronic symptoms. Participants learn strategies to integrate interprofessional collaboration into clinical practice, improving both patient satisfaction and functional recovery in those affected by mechanical back strain.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based clinical guidelines to diagnose and manage mechanical back strain through multimodal conservative therapies, including physical rehabilitation, ergonomic adjustments, and patient education.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Develop patient-specific educational strategies promoting correct posture, lifting techniques, core strengthening, and ergonomic modifications to prevent reinjury.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Compare&#x000a0;the current recommendations for pharmacologic, nonpharmacologic, and invasive treatment options available for mechanical back strain.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate effectively within the interprofessional healthcare team&#x02014;including primary care clinicians, physical therapists, rehabilitation specialists, nurses, and occupational health professionals&#x02014;to ensure coordinated management, timely follow-up, and optimal patient outcomes in mechanical back strain care.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=24813&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=24813">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24813.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Back pain is one of the most frequent chief complaints in the emergency department and a leading cause of disability and healthcare expenditures worldwide, affecting individuals across all ages and backgrounds. Mechanical back strain is a specific subtype of back pain resulting from injury or overuse of the spinal muscles, ligaments, tendons, intervertebral discs, or surrounding soft tissues. The anatomy of the lumbar spine, including vertebrae, intervertebral discs, facet joints, and supporting musculature, plays a critical role in both the development and resolution of mechanical strain. Most mechanical back strains follow a benign natural history, with symptoms resolving over days to weeks with conservative management; however, pain may occasionally radiate along predictable patterns, reflecting the involvement of paraspinal muscles and neural structures. Healthcare&#x000a0;clinicians must remain vigilant for red flags indicating more emergent or serious etiologies, as delayed diagnosis can lead to high morbidity and mortality.<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542314/">[1]</ext-link></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24813.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Historically, mechanical back pain has been described using vague terms such as nonspecific back pain, lumbar strain, or lumbago, reflecting the limited diagnostic tools available in the past. Advances in imaging technology and a deeper understanding of spinal anatomy and innervation have enabled more precise diagnoses, allowing clinicians to identify specific underlying causes. Mechanical back strain typically arises from pathology affecting the spine and its supporting structures. While the lumbar region is most commonly involved, mechanical back pathology may affect any component of the vertebral column, including the bones, ligaments, intervertebral discs, facet joints, spinal cord, spinal nerves, or paraspinal muscles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Common etiologies include spinal foraminal stenosis, herniated discs, spinal stenosis, degenerative disc disease, vertebral fractures, sacroiliac joint dysfunction, facet joint syndrome, ligamentous or muscular injury, and myofascial pain syndrome. Recognition of the specific etiology is essential for guiding targeted management and optimizing patient outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24813.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Chronic low back pain affects approximately 13% of adults in the United States (US). In 2021, an estimated 20.9% of US adults (51.6 million people) experienced chronic pain of any type, with 6.9% (17.1 million) reporting high-impact chronic pain that limits daily activities; low back pain is the most common cause of chronic pain in this population. The annual direct and indirect costs of back pain in the US are estimated at $365 billion, making it one of the most costly health conditions nationally. Among high-utilizers with chronic low back pain, the mean cumulative 5-year back pain-related cost per patient can exceed $31,000, with outpatient services being the primary driver.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24813.s5" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>A thorough patient history and comprehensive physical examination are essential for accurately diagnosing mechanical back strains. In the acute setting, the primary goal is to identify red flag symptoms that may indicate more serious pathology requiring advanced imaging or surgical consultation. Red flag symptoms include fever, unexplained weight loss, night sweats, bladder or bowel incontinence, significant trauma, frequent falls, history of cancer, anticoagulant use, chronic systemic steroid use, intravenous drug use, or immunocompromised status. Physical exam red flags focus on detecting focal neurologic deficits, such as muscle weakness, ataxia, saddle anesthesia, diminished deep tendon reflexes, and decreased rectal tone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Acute mechanical back strains often follow physical activity, particularly lifting, though up to one-third of patients may not recall an inciting event. Specific spinal pathologies present with characteristic signs: herniated discs may cause radicular pain along L4&#x02013;S1 distributions, with pain reproduced on straight leg raise. In contrast, spinal stenosis typically produces back and bilateral leg pain exacerbated by walking or spinal extension and relieved by rest or flexion. Patients with osteoporosis or chronic steroid use are at increased risk for vertebral compression fractures, often presenting with focal tenderness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r1">[1]</xref> Pediatric and adolescent individuals require age-specific evaluation.</p>
        <p>Lumbar spondylolysis, a pars interarticularis fracture, is common among athletes involved in repetitive lumbar extension or rotation activities, presenting as activity-related low back pain, focal tenderness, pain with hyperextension, and hamstring tightness. Progression to low-grade spondylolisthesis may produce similar symptoms, sometimes with a palpable step-off or pain exacerbated by flexion and extension. Other considerations include lumbar muscle or ligamentous strains and posterior element or apophyseal stress injuries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Lumbar muscle or ligamentous strains typically present as localized low back pain aggravated by movement, such as flexion, extension, or rotation, often with paraspinal tenderness, muscle spasm, and limited range of motion, but without neurologic deficits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r5">[5]</xref> Posterior element or apophyseal stress injuries usually cause activity-related low back pain that worsens with extension or rotation, with focal tenderness over the affected vertebrae and pain relief with rest. Imaging may reveal pars interarticularis stress reactions or early spondylolysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24813.s6" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>In the absence of red flags, laboratory or radiographic studies are generally unnecessary for diagnosing or managing acute mechanical back strains. Patients with risk factors for infectious spinal pathology or malignancy but without neurologic deficits may have elevated&#x000a0;erythrocyte sedimentation rate or C-reactive protein&#x000a0;levels that support further evaluation; however, evidence for their use in risk stratification remains limited. Emerging research suggests that elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate, monocyte, and basophil counts may predict chronicity and could inform early imaging decisions in patients with persistent low back pain, though these findings require further validation before routine application. Inflammatory biomarkers are most useful when interpreted collectively and in the context of suspected infection, rather than as a standard screening tool.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Routine imaging, including plain radiographs, computed tomography, or magnetic resonance imaging, is not recommended for nonspecific low back pain in the absence of red flags or neurologic deficits. Imaging may be appropriate for patients who have undergone up to&#x000a0;6 weeks of conservative management, including physical therapy, with minimal improvement. Additionally, imaging is indicated for individuals presenting with red flags suggestive of serious underlying conditions such as cauda equina syndrome, malignancy, fracture, or infection. Plain radiographs and&#x000a0;computed tomography are particularly useful when fractures are suspected. In contrast, magnetic&#x000a0;resonance imaging is preferred for patients with neurologic deficits or signs of spinal cord compression to ensure accurate diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24813.s7" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Management of mechanical back strains depends on the chronicity of symptoms, the patient's comorbidities, and the specific etiology.&#x000a0;According to a review in <italic toggle="yes">The New England Journal of Medicine</italic> (NEJM),&#x000a0;acute low back pain should be managed with patient education, reassurance, and encouragement to maintain normal activities. Nonpharmacological interventions, such as superficial heat, are recommended (moderate-certainty evidence), while massage, acupuncture, and spinal manipulation may be considered (low-certainty evidence). Pharmacologic options include short-term use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs if there are no contraindications. Still, acetaminophen and opioids are not recommended due to a lack of efficacy and potential harm. Weak opioids (tramadol) may be considered for use in carefully selected patients. Imaging is not indicated unless red-flag symptoms are present, and bed rest should be avoided. There was insufficient evidence to determine the benefits of other pharmacologic agents, such as benzodiazepines, antidepressants, antiepileptic medications, and opioids.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Physical therapy is integral in treating mechanical back pain, focusing on pain reduction, restoration of mobility, and prevention of recurrence. Treatment often includes patient-specific exercise programs to improve spinal flexibility, core and paraspinal muscle strength, and postural control. Manual therapy techniques, such as joint mobilization and soft tissue manipulation,&#x000a0;may address soft tissue&#x000a0;mobility limitations or muscular guarding. Education on body mechanics, ergonomics, and activity modification is essential to help patients safely return to daily activities and reduce the risk of reinjury. In chronic or recurrent cases, physical therapists can develop progressive conditioning programs that gradually restore function while minimizing symptom flare-ups, reinforcing self-management strategies to improve&#x000a0;long-term spinal health.</p>
        <p>When initial conservative measures fail, a referral is appropriate. Study results have shown that early physical therapy intervention, when compared to the implementation of physical&#x000a0;therapy after lower back pain had become chronic, resulted&#x000a0;in a reduction in healthcare costs, a decrease in opioid use, and a reduction in spinal surgery rates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Nonpharmacological approaches for chronic low back pain&#x000a0;are&#x000a0;recommended as&#x000a0;first-line agents, including multidisciplinary rehabilitation, Pilates, McKenzie methods, tai chi, yoga, spinal manipulation, acupuncture, psychotherapy, low-level laser therapy, and electromyogram biofeedback. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs&#x000a0;are recommended first-line pharmacologic agents, followed by tramadol and duloxetine as the second-line treatments. Recommendations for opioid therapy are only if the previously mentioned therapies failed and&#x000a0;based on an individualized decision to determine if the benefit outweighs the risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>More invasive procedures are not recommended as first-line therapy for either nonradicular or radicular low back pain or for spinal stenosis. For nonradicular low back pain, the American Society of Pain and Neuroscience guideline states that interventional procedures such as epidural steroid injections, radiofrequency ablation, and diagnostic injections should be reserved for patients who have failed comprehensive nonpharmacologic and pharmacologic management, and only after careful patient selection. The evidence for diagnostic injections in nonradicular pain is limited, and these are not routinely recommended. For chronic nonradicular low back pain, radiofrequency ablation of the medial branch nerves may be considered in select patients with facet-mediated pain, but only after a positive diagnostic block, and with moderate evidence for benefit.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>For radicular low back pain and spinal stenosis, the American Society of Pain and Neuroscience guideline gives a strong recommendation (grade A, high certainty) for the use of interlaminar, transforaminal, or caudal epidural steroid injections for short-term relief of radicular pain due to disc disease or spinal stenosis. Still, it emphasizes that these interventions are not disease-modifying and should be part of a broader multimodal approach. The North American Spine Society and the World Federation of Neurosurgical Societies also support short-term use of epidural injections for radicular pain and spinal stenosis, but note that the addition of steroids to local anesthetics does not confer additional long-term benefit, and that surgery is reserved for patients with persistent, function-limiting symptoms or progressive neurologic deficits. Routine use of invasive procedures for diagnostic purposes is not recommended due to low specificity and limited impact on management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>For surgical management options, standard open discectomy and microdiscectomy have a moderate short-term benefit in treating persistent, debilitating radiculopathy secondary to herniated disc disease in patients who have failed 6 weeks of conservative therapy. Fusion is reserved for cases with documented instability. Results from comparative effectiveness studies show decompression is generally superior to nonoperative care in selected patients, but careful patient selection is essential. For patients with persistent, function-limiting radicular symptoms, such as sciatica, after lumbar disc herniation or surgery, the evidence is mixed. While some network meta-analyses and randomized controlled trials suggest short-term benefit of&#x000a0;spinal cord stimulation over conventional medical management, high-quality placebo-controlled trials have not shown significant improvements in pain, disability, or quality of life compared to sham stimulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24813.s8" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnoses for mechanical back strains include the following:&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Rheumatologic</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Ankylosing spondylitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Reiter syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Psoriatic spondylitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Polymyalgia rheumatica&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Oncologic</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Metastatic disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Spinal cord tumor</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lymphomas</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Leukemia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Multiple myeloma&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Infectious</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Spinal epidural abscess</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Osteomyelitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Discitis&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Gastrointestinal</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pancreatitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cholecystitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Bowel perforation&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Vascular</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Aortic aneurysm</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Spinal epidural hematoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Aortoiliac disease</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Renal</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pyelonephritis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Nephrolithiasis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Perinephric abscess</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Genitourinary</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Endometriosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prostatitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pelvic inflammatory disease&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r20">[20]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24813.s9" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis for patients with low back pain is generally favorable, especially for those with acute or subacute presentations. Most individuals experience substantial improvement in pain and disability within the first 6 weeks, with further gradual improvement over the following months. However, patients with persistent pain beyond 12 weeks are at higher risk for ongoing symptoms and disability, with less pronounced improvement over time. Factors consistently associated with a poorer prognosis include high initial pain intensity, widespread pain, poor physical functioning, negative illness perceptions, passive coping behaviors, and low socioeconomic status.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>According to a review in <italic toggle="yes">NEJM</italic>, the course of low back pain can be categorized into&#x000a0;3 main trajectories: rapid or gradual recovery, ongoing moderate or fluctuating pain, and persistent severe pain. Most patients with acute low back pain (about 70%) follow a recovery trajectory, while this is less common in chronic cases (about 30%), where ongoing or persistent pain is more frequent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;A recent systematic review and meta-analysis confirms that acute and subacute low back pain typically improves substantially within 6 weeks, while persistent low back pain is associated with high pain and disability scores and minimal improvement over time. Another inception cohort study found that 73% of patients with recent-onset low back pain recovered from pain within 12 months, and 86% recovered from disability, with a median time to complete recovery of 70 days. Community-based data suggest that most acute episodes resolve rapidly, with a median duration of 5 days.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r24">[24]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24813.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of low back pain include persistent pain, chronic disability, reduced physical functioning, psychological distress such as depression and anxiety, and increased risk of work absenteeism and socioeconomic impact. Chronicity is a significant complication: about 13% of adults in the&#x000a0;United States have chronic low back pain, and one-third of these experience moderate-to-high-impact pain. Transition from acute to chronic pain occurs in 2% to 48% of cases (median 26%) in primary care settings, with risk factors including high pain intensity, widespread pain, poor physical functioning, high emotional distress, negative recovery expectations, and high physical demands at work.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>Recurrence rates are high; after recovery from an acute episode, 35% to 69% of patients experience a recurrence within 12 months, with a median time to recurrence of 139 days. Recurrence is more likely in those with frequent exposure to awkward postures, prolonged sitting, and a history of multiple previous episodes. In persistent low back pain, acute flare-ups are common, and up to two-thirds of those who recover may relapse within a year.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;Return to work times are generally favorable for most patients with recent-onset low back pain. 79% return to previous work hours and duties within 12 months, with a median time to return to work of 37 days. However, higher pain levels, higher perceived risk of persistent pain, and greater disability are associated with delayed return to work and nonrecovery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24813.s11" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>According to a review in <italic toggle="yes">NEJM</italic>, current guidelines for managing mechanical low back pain emphasize patient education, reassurance, and encouragement to maintain regular activities as key deterrents and preventive strategies. Patients should be informed of the favorable prognosis and the low likelihood of a serious underlying disease. They should be advised to avoid bed rest and to remain as active as possible, even if some pain persists. For those at risk of chronicity, validated tools such as the PICK-UP or &#x000d6;rebro Musculoskeletal Pain Questionnaire can be used to identify patients who may benefit from early referral to supervised exercise or behavioral therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Prevention of recurrence is best supported by evidence from regular exercise, particularly core stabilization and postural training (eg, Pilates), which reduces pain and improves function. Behavioral therapies, including cognitive, operant, and respondent approaches, are also effective in reducing chronicity and promoting self-management. Multidisciplinary interventions that combine physical and psychological components may be especially beneficial for patients with psychosocial risk factors or low functioning.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Return to work should be promoted as early as feasible, with reassurance that activity is safe and beneficial. Education should address misconceptions about pain and disability, encourage active coping, and set realistic expectations for recovery. For patients with persistent symptoms, referral to exercise or behavioral therapy, addressing workplace factors, and providing multidisciplinary support can facilitate reintegration and reduce long-term disability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24813.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Healthcare professionals of many specialties are involved in providing care for patients with mechanical back strains. Primary clinicians&#x000a0;and nurses, especially in the acute setting, must be familiar with red flag symptoms of back pain that may require more&#x000a0;urgent interventions and possible surgical consultation. Conservative management of acute mechanical back strains&#x000a0;after a complete assessment is reasonable, as the course is usually self-limited; this includes superficial heat, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and muscle relaxants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Due to the psychosocial factors involved, chronic mechanical back strains require an interprofessional team approach.&#x000a0;Initial nonpharmacologic&#x000a0;therapy includes exercise, interprofessional rehabilitation, manipulation, and acupuncture. First-line pharmacologic therapy is nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, followed by duloxetine&#x000a0;or tramadol as second-line therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;A pharmacist should review the patient's medications to ensure no drug interactions and that dosing is appropriate. Patients with persistent, disabling chronic nonradicular low back pain may benefit from interprofessional rehabilitation, described as rehabilitation along with a psychological, social, or occupational component.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In this setting, the orthopedic specialty nurse is key in coordinating therapy, monitoring patient progress, addressing patient concerns, and communicating with the managing clinician if further intervention is warranted. Patients with persistent chronic radicular pain due to herniated discs or spinal stenosis may benefit from surgical interventions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24813.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Mechanical back pain requires an interprofessional team approach, including primary clinicians, specialists, specialty-trained nurses, physical therapists, chiropractors, and pharmacists, collaborating across disciplines to achieve optimal patient results.</p>
      </sec>
